# Example 42: Ecosystem evolution (invasion-fitness loop, doc/19 §5.3)
# A chemostat consumer with a real DNA genotype (36 nt) runs the
# evolution loop: each generation the genome is mutated (evolution.py
# substitution spectrum), the mutant is evaluated against the resident on
# its growth in the *same* environment the population is itself changing
# (resident_growth vs mutant_growth), and a fitter mutant substitutes.
# The resident/mutant comparison is largest in generation 0 (fresh
# glucose); later generations converge on the flow-supported steady state
# and occasional substitutions still flip `substituted` to 1.  The final
# genome differs from the seed `ATGCTA`x6 - a genome that changed in
# response to an environment it also changed.

#species name=consumer substrate=glucose vmax=0.02 ks=0.1 cn_ratio=6 maintenance=0.0015
ATG CTA ATG CTA ATG CTA
ATG CTA ATG CTA ATG CTA
#end

#patch name=c kind=chemostat flow_rate=0.005 anoxic=true initial.consumer=50 initial_nh4_mm=50 substrate.glucose.initial=50 substrate.glucose.bulk=50

#config backend=ecosystem

#sim kind=ecosystem ticks=0 seed=7 fast_forward=true
#sim generations=6 evaluation_ticks=50 substitution_rate=0.05 genome_length=36
#sim output=generation,tick,consumer:resident_growth,consumer:mutant_growth,consumer:substituted
